0 < 1 """The plan-check exams: every test that drives
1 < 1 `skills/ultrapowers/scripts/plan_check.py`, in one file, each shared helper
3 > 1 defined once. Three sections, in order:
2 < 2 defined once. Three sections, in order: GREEN-AT-BASE, the run-wide freeze,
3 < 2 and the authoring record.
4 < 1 
6 > 1   * GREEN-AT-BASE (#1098): with `--base`, every Proof `Run:` is rehearsed in a
7 < 1     detached worktree at BASE, and each one already green prints a
8 < 1     `GREEN-AT-BASE fact:` line, plus one reading of what the rehearsal cost.
9 < 1     A fact, never a refusal: the verdict stays `PLAN OK`.
10 < 1   * The run-wide freeze (#1202): a `Check:` freeze whose pathspec covers a
11 < 1     task's own Files is refused (`freeze_violations`).
12 < 1   * The authoring record: with `--base`, the gate record's `authoring` object
13 < 1     prints one `AUTHORING fact:` line (minutes, gate dispatches and
14 < 1     rejections, routing, question counts), each unsaid field read as `-`;
15 < 1     the record is telemetry and never refuses (#1440).
5 < 2 === GREEN-AT-BASE ===
6 < 1 
8 > 1 The direct legs import plan_check and call its functions; the subprocess legs
9 < 1 write a real claims-v1 plan (inside a one-commit git repository when they
10 < 1 need a BASE), sign its gate-verdict record with the gate's own extractor, and
11 < 1 read the exit code and both output channels.
7 < 2 GREEN-AT-BASE: the compiler rehearses every Proof `Run:` at BASE (#1098).
8 < 2 
9 < 2 A `Run:` line that already passes at BASE proves nothing about the task —
10 < 2 whatever the implementer writes, that line was green before they started. So
11 < 2 `plan_check.py --base <sha>` cuts a detached worktree at BASE,
12 < 2 runs every Proof `Run:` there, and prints one `GREEN-AT-BASE fact:` line per
13 < 2 command that exits 0, plus one reading line saying what the rehearsal cost.
14 < 2 This release the lines are a fact and never a refusal: the verdict stays
15 < 2 `PLAN OK` and the exit stays 0.
16 < 2 
17 < 2 The seven Machine clauses, restated, and where each is graded here:
18 < 2 
19 < 2   * M1 — a Proof `Run:` bullet whose value ends in a citation tag (`[M2]`,
20 < 2     `[M1, M3]`) is a PROVER: `parse_task` records the command WITHOUT the tag
21 < 2     in `proof_runs` and the tag's clause ids, sorted, in a parallel
22 < 2     `proof_run_cites` list; an untagged `Run:` is a GUARD, recorded there as
23 < 2     `[]`. Graded by leg (a), which calls `parse_task` directly and compares
24 < 2     both lists by equality.
25 < 2   * M2 — a tag naming a clause the Machine line does not number is refused at
26 < 2     `--check` with
27 < 2 
28 < 2         grammar: Run: cites an unknown clause — task <id>: <command cut to 80 characters> cites <Mn>; the Machine line numbers <span>
29 < 2 
30 < 2     and a tag never counts as a citing leg: a clause cited only by a `Run:`
31 < 2     tag still draws `Machine clause <Mn> has no citing Proof leg`. Graded by
32 < 2     leg (b), both halves, as whole-line strings off a real `--check`.
33 < 2   * M3 — `green_at_base_lines(tasks, base_tree, timeout_s=GREEN_AT_BASE_TIMEOUT_S)`
34 < 2     cuts ONE detached worktree of the plan's repository at BASE's commit under
35 < 2     a fresh temporary directory, runs every task's `Run:` commands there in
36 < 2     Proof order through `bash -lc` with `ULTRA_BASE` set to the 40-hex commit
37 < 2     and the worktree as working directory, and removes the worktree afterwards
38 < 2     whatever the commands did. Graded by leg (c) — the command itself asserts
39 < 2     where it ran, and the exam asserts what is left behind.
40 < 2   * M4 — the three outcome lines, verbatim: a prover that exits 0, a guard
41 < 2     that exits 0, and nothing at all for a command that exits non-zero. Graded
42 < 2     by leg (d), by equality, from the function and from the `--check --base`
43 < 2     stdout alike.
44 < 2   * M5 — a command still running at `timeout_s` seconds (`GREEN_AT_BASE_TIMEOUT_S
45 < 2     = 30` by default) is killed with its whole process group, yields the
46 < 2     `not run (timeout after <timeout_s> s)` line, and the call returns within
47 < 2     3 seconds of the limit. Graded by leg (e), which passes `timeout_s=1` so
48 < 2     the suite never waits 30 seconds, and by leg (h)'s first `Run:` line.
49 < 2   * M6 — the last line the function returns is the reading,
50 < 2     `GREEN-AT-BASE fact: <S> s over <R> lines run, <T> not run (timeout)`,
51 < 2     returned even for a plan with no `Run:` line at all. Graded by leg (f),
52 < 2     all three shapes.
53 < 2   * M7 — `--check --base <base>` whose verdict is `PLAN OK` prints every line
54 < 2     of M4–M6 after the `BASE fact:`/`STALE fact:` lines and before the
55 < 2     `AUTHORING fact:` line, still prints `PLAN OK` first and still exits 0; a
56 < 2     bare `--check`, a plain compile with or without `--base`, and a
57 < 2     `--check --base` whose verdict is a refusal run no command and print no
58 < 2     `GREEN-AT-BASE fact:` line. Graded by leg (g), whose witness is a `Run:`
59 < 2     line that touches a file outside the worktree.
60 < 2 
61 < 2 Leg (h) is the Proof's `Run:` lines. The constant at 30 is graded here; the
62 < 2 function's signature is exercised by every leg that calls it. Its third is `python3 -m pytest -q
63 < 2 tests/test_plan_check.py`, which is THIS file: an exam never
64 < 2 runs an exam, least of all itself, so the driver runs that line and nothing
65 < 2 here does.
66 < 2 
67 < 2 The shape is `tests/test_compile_plan_edges.py` and
68 < 2 `tests/test_compile_plan_exam_sweep.py`: the direct legs import `compile_plan`
69 < 2 from `skills/ultrapowers/scripts` and call the function; the `--check` legs
70 < 2 write a real claims-v1 plan inside a one-commit git repository under
71 < 2 `tmp_path`, sign its gate-verdict record with the gate's own extractor, run the
72 < 2 compiler as a subprocess, and read the exit code and the two output channels.
73 < 2 
74 < 2 === The run-wide freeze ===
75 < 2 
76 < 2 `plan_check.py` refuses a run-wide `Check:` freeze that covers a task's own
77 < 2 Files (#1202).
78 < 2 
79 < 2 Machine, restated, and where each clause is graded here:
80 < 2 
81 < 2   * M1 — a signed one-task plan whose `## Global Constraints` carries
82 < 2     `- Check: git diff --quiet $ULTRA_BASE -- fleet/`, with task 1 naming
83 < 2     `fleet/tests/test_x.mjs` as its `Create:` path and `src/prover.ts` as its
84 < 2     `Modify:` path, exits 2 under a bare `plan_check.py <plan>` (no `--base`),
85 < 2     prints no `PLAN OK`, and prints exactly one line carrying `freezes` — that
86 < 2     line also carries the check's whole command, the pathspec `fleet/`,
87 < 2     `task 1` and the path `fleet/tests/test_x.mjs`. Graded by leg (a),
88 < 2     subprocess, and again directly against `freeze_violations` in leg (d).
89 < 2   * M2 — the same plan with the check
90 < 2     `git diff --quiet $ULTRA_BASE -- fleet/launch.mjs fleet/te` prints
91 < 2     `PLAN OK`, exits 0, and carries no `freezes` line — `fleet/te` is a
92 < 2     string prefix of the task's path, not a directory prefix, so it covers
93 < 2     nothing. Graded by leg (b), and again directly in leg (d).
94 < 2   * M3 — the same plan with the check
95 < 2     `git diff --quiet $ULTRA_BASE -- docs src/prover.ts` exits 2 and prints a
96 < 2     line carrying `freezes`, `src/prover.ts` and `task 1` — a pathspec equal
97 < 2     to a task's path covers it. Graded by leg (c), and again directly in leg
98 < 2     (d).
99 < 2   * M4 — `freeze_violations(checks, tasks)` is a module-level function of
100 < 2     `plan_check.py` that answers from the `checks`/`tasks` values alone.
101 < 2     Graded by leg (d): its answering M1/M2/M3 correctly when
102 < 2     called directly on plain dicts (no `tmp_path`, no git, no subprocess —
103 < 2     demonstrating it needs none). `main` summing its
104 < 2     answer into `violations` is what legs (a)-(c) already exercise end to
105 < 2     end; that the function itself spawns nothing is a diff-reading claim (the
106 < 2     Context's own words), not one this file can observe from outside.
107 < 2 
108 < 2 The shape is the GREEN-AT-BASE section's: a claims-v1 plan is built
109 < 2 directly (no repository — this task runs `plan_check.py` with no `--base`,
110 < 2 so none is needed) and signed with the gate's own extractor, then run as a
111 < 2 subprocess so stdout and the exit code are read as the compiler actually
112 < 2 prints them.
113 < 2 
114 < 2 === The authoring record ===
115 < 2 
116 < 2 The authoring record: one AUTHORING fact line, and a refusal when it is
117 < 2 malformed (#988 desired state 1).
118 < 2 
119 < 2 The record sits at the top level of `<plan-stem>.gate-verdicts.json` beside
120 < 2 `tasks` and `tally`, and it is the one literal every task of this plan shares:
121 < 2 
122 < 2     {"authoring": {"minutes": 118, "probes": 12,
123 < 2                    "routing": {"branch": "risk", "lane": "ultrapowers"},
124 < 2                    "questions": [{"question": "Claim and summary",
125 < 2                                   "options": ["A", "B"],
126 < 2                                   "recommended": "A", "picked": "A"}]}}
127 < 2 
128 < 2 Three clauses, one exam:
129 < 2 
130 < 2   * M1 — under `--check --base <sha>`, a well-formed record prints, after the
131 < 2     verdict line, exactly one `AUTHORING fact:` line carrying the sitting's
132 < 2     minutes, hub probes, the `tally`'s dispatches and rejections (`-` for an
133 < 2     absent key), the routing branch and lane, and the question counts; the
134 < 2     compile still exits 0 with `PLAN OK`.
135 < 2   * M2 — a record with no `authoring` key prints `AUTHORING fact: none
136 < 2     recorded` under the same flags, and a bare `--check` of either record
137 < 2     prints no `AUTHORING fact:` line at all.
138 < 2   * M3 — a malformed `authoring` object is refused at `--check`: exit 2, no
139 < 2     `PLAN OK`, one `grammar: authoring record unreadable —` line naming the
140 < 2     offending field.
141 < 2 
142 < 2 Every plan below is a real claims-v1 plan written to a temp directory and
143 < 2 compiled by a subprocess, its gate verdicts hashed by the gate's own extractor,
144 < 2 so nothing here reaches into the compiler's internals. The `--base` legs put
145 < 2 that plan inside a one-commit git repository, because a 40-hex `--base` is
146 < 2 resolved in the plan's own toplevel.
147 < 1 """
